picolibc 1.8.12's libos/linux _exit() calls SYS_exit, which on Linux ends
only the CALLING THREAD. POSIX says a return from main, or exit(), ends
the whole program: every other thread goes too. Without this a program
that returns from main leaves its threads running, belonging to a
process that has finished.

Not specific to m68k. glibc, musl and every other Linux C library call
exit_group here.

diff -ru a/libos/linux/_exit.c b/libos/linux/_exit.c
--- a/libos/linux/_exit.c	2026-09-22 14:50:51.950407668 -0600
+++ b/libos/linux/_exit.c	2026-09-22 14:50:51.967423440 -0600
@@ -35,9 +35,20 @@
 
 #include "local-linux.h"
 
+/*
+ * exit_group, not exit: this ends the PROCESS.
+ *
+ * POSIX says a return from main, or exit(), ends the program and every
+ * thread in it. syscall(exit) ends only the calling thread, so a
+ * program whose last act was to return from main left its threads
+ * running with nothing to run for -- visible as tasks belonging to a
+ * process that had finished. Linux's C libraries all call exit_group
+ * here for this reason; pthread_exit is the call that ends one thread.
+ */
 void
 _exit(int status)
 {
-    syscall(LINUX_SYS_exit, status);
+    syscall(LINUX_SYS_exit_group, status);
+    syscall(LINUX_SYS_exit, status);    /* a kernel without it */
     __builtin_unreachable();
 }
